(6685463, 997593, 67152, '<td valign="top">While you did a great job of capturing the feel of a club with the terrific lighting and overall composition, this photo is driving me absolutely bonkers!!!!  The model does not appear to have any idea how to hold the instrument - or was instructed to hold it this strange and unplayable way.  As a musician, this is the type of stuff that drives me batty!  <br/> <br/>Congrats on the ribbon - it is clear that the photo struck a chord with folks here at DPC for the look of the scene.  I just wish people knew a bit more about stuff like this...as it is clear that live music is becoming a lost thing.<br/> <br/></td>', 'While you did a great job of capturing the feel of a club with the terrific lighting and overall composition, this photo is driving me absolutely bonkers!!!!  The model does not appear to have any idea how to hold the instrument - or was instructed to hold it this strange and unplayable way.  As a musician, this is the type of stuff that drives me batty!   Congrats on the ribbon - it is clear that the photo struck a chord with folks here at DPC for the look of the scene.  I just wish people knew a bit more about stuff like this...as it is clear that live music is becoming a lost thing. ', '2012-02-20 18:42:59.000000', NULL, 0),
(6685467, 998647, 33068, '<td valign="top">Pretty clever if you ask me.  Nice job.</td>', 'Pretty clever if you ask me.  Nice job.', '2012-02-20 18:49:16.000000', NULL, 0),
(6685593, 997593, 112972, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by bassbone:</b></div><hr/><i> While you did a great job of capturing the feel of a club with the terrific lighting and overall composition, this photo is driving me absolutely bonkers!!!!  The model does not appear to have any idea how to hold the instrument - or was instructed to hold it this strange and unplayable way.  As a musician, this is the type of stuff that drives me batty!  <br/> <br/>Congrats on the ribbon - it is clear that the photo struck a chord with folks here at DPC for the look of the scene.  I just wish people knew a bit more about stuff like this...as it is clear that live music is becoming a lost thing. </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>While I appreciate the input of a musician, keep in mind that this is a photography contest and the most important thing to me and most any other photographer is going for the lighting, composition, and the mood they are aiming for.  Holding the instrument in such a way that is considered to be the correct way by a musician was certainly not my goal especially since I had only 10 minutes to shoot during my busy schedule.  In an ideal world, I would have loved to photograph an actual Sax player, if I had the time to look for one but life can sometimes get quite busy and we just have to do whatever we can in the shortest amount of time possible.  If you are ever in the Los Angeles area while I am in town too and you own a Sax, I would be more than happy to photograph you while you are playing your sax the "correct" way.<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2012-02-21 01:14:07.</i></td>', 'Originally posted by bassbone: While you did a great job of capturing the feel of a club with the terrific lighting and overall composition, this photo is driving me absolutely bonkers!!!!  The model does not appear to have any idea how to hold the instrument - or was instructed to hold it this strange and unplayable way.  As a musician, this is the type of stuff that drives me batty!   Congrats on the ribbon - it is clear that the photo struck a chord with folks here at DPC for the look of the scene.  I just wish people knew a bit more about stuff like this...as it is clear that live music is becoming a lost thing.   While I appreciate the input of a musician, keep in mind that this is a photography contest and the most important thing to me and most any other photographer is going for the lighting, composition, and the mood they are aiming for.  Holding the instrument in such a way that is considered to be the correct way by a musician was certainly not my goal especially since I had only 10 minutes to shoot during my busy schedule.  In an ideal world, I would have loved to photograph an actual Sax player, if I had the time to look for one but life can sometimes get quite busy and we just have to do whatever we can in the shortest amount of time possible.  If you are ever in the Los Angeles area while I am in town too and you own a Sax, I would be more than happy to photograph you while you are playing your sax the "correct" way.', '2012-02-20 21:22:02.000000', '2012-02-21 01:14:07.000000', 0),
(6685609, 998609, 49018, '<td valign="top">When I saw this one I thought of the typical improvisational devices of jazz music. Repetition with variations, entering and leaving phrases not from the start but from the end and backwards, or somewhere in the middle, shortening and dilating the same phrase. Which is big part of the concept of exploration in so many genres. Django Reinhardt used to play the same note in sequences for whole measures, each note different as for dynamic and timbre, a one note melody.<br/>With this image, I feel, you have illustrated the main points of a improvisation theory text book, in one frame and 1/60 of second.<br/>Postprocessing is really good indeed, I was hoping that would help score-wise, but perhaps it doesn''t matter.<br/> <br/>Thanks for that, these are hard to come by, here and elsewhere.</td>', 'When I saw this one I thought of the typical improvisational devices of jazz music. Repetition with variations, entering and leaving phrases not from the start but from the end and backwards, or somewhere in the middle, shortening and dilating the same phrase. Which is big part of the concept of exploration in so many genres. Django Reinhardt used to play the same note in sequences for whole measures, each note different as for dynamic and timbre, a one note melody.With this image, I feel, you have illustrated the main points of a improvisation theory text book, in one frame and 1/60 of second.Postprocessing is really good indeed, I was hoping that would help score-wise, but perhaps it doesn''t matter. Thanks for that, these are hard to come by, here and elsewhere.', '2012-02-20 21:36:19.000000', NULL, 0),
